-### Method Steps
-1. **Render Depth and Opacity**: For each training view, render the depth map and opacity map using standard 3DGS rasterization [G]. The depth for a pixel is the weighted average of the distances of the splatted Gaussians along the ray [G].
-2. **Self-Regularization Loss**: Introduce a loss term $\mathcal{L}_{align}$ to constrain the distance between the camera and the closest Gaussian to match the pixel's depth value in the rendered depth map [S1]. Let $d_{ray}$ be the rendered depth at a pixel and $d_{closest}$ be the distance from the camera center to the center of the closest Gaussian intersecting the ray [S1]. The loss is defined as:
-   $$ \mathcal{L}_{align} = \sum_{v} \sum_{p \in \text{pixels}_v} | d_{ray}(p) - d_{closest}(p) | $$
-   This encourages the Gaussians to align with the authentic 3D surface [S1].
-3. **Flatness and Alignment Penalty**: Apply a penalty term to make Gaussians flat and aligned with the surface normal [S3, unofficial]. This regularizes the scale vector to be small in the direction perpendicular to the surface normal, effectively flattening the ellipsoids into surfel-like primitives [S3, unofficial].
-4. **Optimization**: Optimize the Gaussian parameters (positions, scales, rotations) by minimizing the sum of the standard 3DGS photometric loss and the new alignment/flatness losses [G].
-5. **Poisson Mesh Reconstruction**: Extract points and normals from the optimized Gaussians [S3, unofficial]. Points are taken from the Gaussian centers and normals from the rotation-derived surface normals of the flattened Gaussians [S3, unofficial]. Run Poisson surface reconstruction on these samples to generate a watertight mesh [S3, unofficial].
+1.  **Render Depth and Opacity:**
+    Render the current 3DGS model to obtain a depth map $D$ and an opacity map $\alpha$ for each input view. The depth $d_i$ for a pixel is the weighted average of the distances of the splatted Gaussians along the ray [S1].
 
